-- Q11397: State and Local Government [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under GASB 54, paragraph 30, a special revenue fund accounts for proceeds of specific revenue sources that are restricted or committed to expenditure for specified purposes. When the state legislature repeals the fuel tax allocation, the specific revenue source that justified the fund is eliminated, so the fund should be closed or reclassified.
Wrong (A): A special revenue fund cannot continue reporting simply because it was ''properly established.'' The ongoing existence of the restricted or committed revenue source is the defining criterion, not the original establishment.
Wrong (C): There is no automatic conversion to a permanent fund. Permanent funds hold resources whose principal must be preserved, which is unrelated to a lapsed revenue source.
Wrong (D): A positive fund balance from prior years does not justify continuing a special revenue fund. Without the specific restricted or committed revenue source, the fund no longer meets GASB 54 criteria.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11397;

-- Q11399: State and Local Government [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under GASB 91, paragraphs 8-9, conduit debt obligations are not reported as liabilities of the issuing government because the third party (Greenfield Hospital) is the primary obligor. The issuer must disclose outstanding conduit debt in the notes. The separate $1,440,000 commitment is evaluated independently and recognized as a liability to the extent payment is probable.
Wrong (A): The additional commitment does not transform the conduit debt into the Authority''s own obligation. The $18,000,000 remains conduit debt disclosed in the notes, not reported as long-term debt.
Wrong (B): While the conduit debt is note-disclosed, the $1,440,000 commitment requires separate liability recognition when probable, not merely related-party disclosure.
Wrong (D): The Authority does bear risk through its reserve pledge commitment. Ignoring both the note disclosure and the commitment liability understates the Authority''s obligations.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11399;

-- Q11402: State and Local Government [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under GASB 34 (par. 41), interfund reimbursements are not reported as transfers or revenues. The paying fund reduces its expenditures and the receiving fund records the expenditure, so the net effect is a reclassification within governmental activities. Total governmental activities expenses remain unchanged because the reimbursement merely redirects which function bears the cost.
Wrong (B): Total expenses do not decrease. The reimbursement reallocates the expense from general government to parks but does not eliminate it from governmental activities.
Wrong (C): Total expenses do not increase. The reimbursement is not a separate expense; it offsets the original charge so only one $175,000 expenditure appears across all functions.
Wrong (D): A $350,000 decrease is impossible. Only $175,000 was involved, and reimbursements reclassify rather than eliminate expenses.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11402;

-- Q11404: State and Local Government [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under GASB 34 (par. 67-68), an enterprise fund is required when fees are charged to external users as the primary revenue source or when legally required. Since 80% of Ridgecrest''s fleet billings remain internal, the fund''s predominant purpose is still internal service delivery. The external activity should be disclosed, but reclassification is not required.
Wrong (B): GASB 34 does not mandate enterprise fund classification merely because some external billing exists. The threshold is whether external users are the primary revenue source, not any external activity.
Wrong (C): There is no ''de minimis threshold'' in GASB 34 for external billings. At 20%, external activity is a minority, and a trend alone does not trigger mandatory reclassification.
Wrong (D): Reclassification does not require a formal governing body resolution. Fund classification is based on the criteria in GASB 34, not on procedural formalities.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11404;

-- Q11405: State and Local Government [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under GASB standards, the purchases method records the full $420,000 as expenditures when purchased. If ending inventory is significant, the balance sheet reports a $130,000 inventory asset with a corresponding nonspendable fund balance reserve. Spendable fund balance is $130,000 lower than under the consumption method, which would expense only $290,000.
Wrong (A): Fund balance is lower, not higher, under the purchases method. The full purchase price is expensed, and the nonspendable reserve for inventory restricts rather than increases spendable fund balance.
Wrong (B): Fund balance is not the same. The purchases method expenses the full $420,000 up front, while the consumption method expenses only $290,000, creating a $130,000 difference in spendable fund balance.
Wrong (D): The purchases method does report significant inventory on the balance sheet with a nonspendable offset under GASB 54, so fund balance is not $420,000 lower.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11405;

-- Q11406: State and Local Government [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under GASB 68 (par. 57), employer contributions made after the measurement date but before the reporting date are reported as deferred outflows of resources. These amounts are recognized as a reduction of net pension liability in the next measurement period, not in the current period.
Wrong (A): The contribution does not reduce the current period''s net pension liability because the liability is measured as of September 30, before the November 15 payment was made.
Wrong (C): A deferred outflow of resources is a distinct financial statement element under GASB 63, not a prepaid asset. Classifying it as prepaid would misstate the statement of net position.
Wrong (D): The contribution is not expensed in full as current pension expense. GASB 68 requires it to be deferred and recognized in the subsequent measurement period.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11406;

-- Q11407: State and Local Government [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under GASB 34 (par. 48-52), program revenues include charges for services and program-specific grants. Building permits ($1,350,000) and court fines ($640,000) are charges for services, and the federal public safety grant ($2,800,000) is a program-specific grant, totaling $4,790,000.
Wrong (A): $37,350,000 incorrectly includes property taxes and other general revenues. Property taxes support the government as a whole and are classified as general revenues under GASB 34.
Wrong (B): $41,450,000 includes all listed revenues. Unrestricted state revenue sharing and investment income are general revenues, not program revenues.
Wrong (D): $8,890,000 incorrectly adds unrestricted state revenue sharing ($4,100,000) to the program revenue total. Unrestricted intergovernmental revenue is a general revenue.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11407;

-- Q11408: State and Local Government [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under GASB 34 (par. 34-36), restricted net position includes resources constrained by external parties or by law. The state highway grant ($2,800,000) is restricted by the grantor, and debt service reserves ($1,400,000) are restricted by bond covenants, totaling $4,200,000.
Wrong (A): $5,150,000 incorrectly adds the assigned amount ($950,000) to the restricted total. Assigned resources reflect internal intent, not external constraints, and are classified as unrestricted.
Wrong (C): $8,350,000 includes both committed ($3,200,000) and assigned ($950,000) amounts. Internal constraints like council commitments and management assignments are unrestricted on government-wide statements.
Wrong (D): $2,800,000 omits the debt service reserves required by bond covenants. Bond covenant restrictions are external constraints that qualify as restricted net position.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11408;

-- Q11411: State and Local Government [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under GASB 87 and GASB 34 (par. 100-102), amortization of the right-of-use asset is an operating expense because it represents the cost of using the asset in operations. Interest on the lease liability is a nonoperating expense, consistent with how all interest costs are classified in proprietary funds.
Wrong (A): Interest on the lease liability is not an operating expense. GASB 34 classifies interest costs as nonoperating regardless of whether the underlying asset supports operations.
Wrong (C): GASB 87 requires the asset-liability model, not rental expense treatment. The lease is bifurcated into amortization and interest, not recorded as a single operating payment.
Wrong (D): Amortization of the right-of-use asset is an operating expense because it reflects the consumption of an asset used in service delivery, not a financing cost.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11411;

-- Q11412: State and Local Government [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under GASB standards, when encumbrances lapse at year-end, the outstanding encumbrance entry is reversed by debiting budgetary fund balance—reserve for encumbrances and crediting encumbrances for $245,000. The goods have not been received, so no expenditure or liability is recorded. The purchase order must be re-encumbered in the next fiscal year.
Wrong (A): No liability exists because the goods have not been received. Accruing an accounts payable would overstate liabilities and prematurely recognize expenditures.
Wrong (B): Reclassifying to committed fund balance is not the correct year-end entry when encumbrances lapse. The encumbrance must be reversed, not reclassified.
Wrong (C): Automatic carryforward describes non-lapsing encumbrance treatment. Under a lapsing policy, the encumbrance entry must be actively reversed at year-end.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11412;

-- Q11415: State and Local Government [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under GASB 33, fines are imposed nonexchange revenues. Under modified accrual, revenue is recognized when measurable and available. The $410,000 collected plus $80,000 expected within the 60-day availability period totals $490,000. The $70,000 deemed uncollectible is excluded.
Wrong (A): $560,000 includes the full levy without adjusting for collectibility or availability. Modified accrual requires both measurability and availability, excluding the uncollectible and unavailable portions.
Wrong (B): $410,000 omits the $80,000 expected within the availability period. Amounts expected to be collected within 60 days meet the available criterion and must be recognized.
Wrong (D): $480,000 appears to subtract only the uncollectible $70,000 from the assessed total without applying the 60-day availability criterion to the remaining balance.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11415;

-- Q11420: State and Local Government [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under GASB 34, a permanent fund accounts for resources legally restricted so that only earnings—not principal—may be used for the government''s own programs. Because the endowment benefits the city''s library programs (a government purpose), it belongs in a permanent fund.
Wrong (A): A special revenue fund accounts for restricted revenue streams, not endowment principal that must be permanently preserved. The defining feature here is the principal preservation requirement.
Wrong (B): A private-purpose trust fund would apply if the beneficiaries were external parties rather than government programs. Since the earnings fund public library programs, a permanent fund is appropriate.
Wrong (C): The general fund is for unrestricted resources. This endowment has a legally imposed restriction on principal that requires a separate fund classification.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11420;

-- Q11423: State and Local Government [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under GASB 65 (par. 12), bond issuance costs, except prepaid insurance, are recognized as an expense (proprietary funds) or expenditure (governmental funds) in the period incurred. The $180,000 is a capital projects fund expenditure and the $95,000 is an enterprise fund expense, both in the current period.
Wrong (A): GASB 65 eliminated the practice of amortizing issuance costs in proprietary funds. The $95,000 is expensed in the current period, not amortized over the bond term.
Wrong (B): Capitalizing issuance costs as deferred charges was the pre-GASB 65 treatment. GASB 65 specifically prohibits deferral and amortization of these costs.
Wrong (D): Netting issuance costs against bond proceeds is not permitted under GASB 65. The $180,000 is reported as a separate expenditure in the capital projects fund.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11423;

-- Q11424: State and Local Government [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under GASB 34 (par. 55-56), a special item is a transaction within management''s control that is either unusual or infrequent. The council-approved, one-time sale qualifies as a special item and must be presented separately on the statement of activities, after general revenues but before the change in net position.
Wrong (A): The gain is not a program revenue. Program revenues are charges for services or program-specific grants, not proceeds from one-time asset disposals approved by management.
Wrong (C): While asset disposals are not program-specific, this transaction qualifies as a special item due to its unusual or infrequent nature and management control. It cannot be buried in general revenues.
Wrong (D): GASB 56 eliminated extraordinary items from governmental accounting. There is no extraordinary item classification available under current GASB standards.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11424;

-- Q11427: State and Local Government [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under GASB 54 (par. 18), the default spending order is restricted first, then committed, then assigned, then unassigned. However, GASB 54 permits governments to adopt a formal spending policy that changes this order. Eastgate''s policy specifies committed before restricted, so $2,200,000 committed is spent first, then $2,300,000 of restricted, totaling $4,500,000.
Wrong (A): This follows the default GASB 54 order, ignoring the city''s adopted spending policy that prioritizes committed before restricted resources.
Wrong (B): This incorrectly inserts assigned resources ($800,000) between committed and restricted. Under the city''s policy, committed is spent first, then restricted—assigned resources are consumed only after both.
Wrong (C): Starting with assigned resources contradicts both the default GASB 54 order and the city''s adopted policy. Assigned is the lowest-priority classification before unassigned.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11427;

-- Q11429: State and Local Government [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under GASB 34 (par. 100-102), the $2,600,000 construction grant is a capital contribution reported after nonoperating items because it funds capital asset acquisition. The $420,000 operating grant supports ongoing testing and is reported as nonoperating revenue, since grants are generally classified as nonoperating even when supporting operations.
Wrong (A): Grants are generally not classified as operating revenues in proprietary funds. Operating revenues arise from the fund''s principal ongoing operations, such as user charges, not from intergovernmental grants.
Wrong (B): This reverses the correct classification. The $2,600,000 capital grant is the capital contribution, not the $420,000 operating grant that supports routine testing activities.
Wrong (C): The $420,000 operating grant does not fund capital asset acquisition and should not be classified as a capital contribution. Only grants restricted for capital purposes qualify.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11429;

-- Q11430: State and Local Government [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under GASB 34 (par. 22-26), capital assets with indefinite or inexhaustible useful lives are not depreciated. Land has an inexhaustible useful life, and construction in progress is not yet placed in service, so neither is subject to depreciation.
Wrong (B): Buildings and improvements have finite useful lives and must be depreciated systematically over those lives under GASB 34.
Wrong (C): Infrastructure assets reported under the depreciation approach are, by definition, being depreciated. Only infrastructure under the modified approach may avoid depreciation if condition levels are maintained.
Wrong (D): Equipment and vehicles have determinable useful lives and are depreciated over their estimated service periods.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11430;

-- Q11431: State and Local Government [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under GASB 40 (par. 8), custodial credit risk for deposits is the risk that in the event of a depository institution failure, the government''s deposits may not be returned because they are uninsured and uncollateralized. This focuses specifically on the absence of depository insurance or collateral protection.
Wrong (A): Interest rate risk is the risk that fair value of investments will decline due to rising interest rates. This is a separate risk category under GASB 40, distinct from custodial credit risk.
Wrong (B): This describes general credit risk or counterparty risk rather than the specific custodial credit risk concept, which focuses on the absence of insurance and collateralization coverage.
Wrong (D): Concentration risk refers to overexposure to a single issuer. GASB 40 treats concentration of credit risk as a separate disclosure requirement from custodial credit risk.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11431;

-- Q11432: State and Local Government [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under GASB Interpretation No. 1, bond anticipation notes that meet take-out financing criteria—meaning the government has entered into a financing agreement to convert them to long-term debt—are excluded from governmental fund liabilities and reported only in the government-wide statements as long-term debt. If criteria are unmet, the BANs remain a fund liability.
Wrong (A): Governmental funds do not report long-term liabilities on the fund balance sheet. Long-term debt appears only in the government-wide statements, not in fund-level reporting.
Wrong (C): BANs are not automatically classified as other financing sources. They must first be evaluated against take-out criteria to determine proper classification.
Wrong (D): BANs are debt obligations, not deferred inflows of resources. Deferred inflows represent resources acquired but not yet earned, which does not describe borrowing proceeds.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11432;

-- Q11433: State and Local Government [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under GASB 33 (par. 7), voluntary nonexchange transactions result from legislative or contractual agreements that are not imposed on the provider or recipient. Examples include grants, donations, and entitlements where both parties enter the arrangement willingly.
Wrong (A): A transaction imposed by a higher-level government describes a government-mandated nonexchange transaction, not a voluntary one. The key distinction is whether participation is compelled or willing.
Wrong (B): A property tax levy is an imposed nonexchange transaction, not voluntary. The government unilaterally assesses the tax on property owners without their individual consent.
Wrong (C): A transaction derived from an underlying exchange between a taxpayer and a third party describes a derived tax revenue (such as sales or income taxes), a separate category under GASB 33.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11433;

-- Q11434: State and Local Government [hard/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under GASB 18 (par. 8-9), the closure and postclosure care liability is calculated based on the percentage of landfill capacity used: (2,600,000 / 4,000,000) x $18,000,000 = $11,700,000. The restricted investments are reported separately and do not offset the liability.
Wrong (B): $7,500,000 incorrectly subtracts the $4,200,000 in restricted investments from the computed liability. GASB 18 requires the liability and restricted assets to be reported independently on the statement of net position.
Wrong (C): $18,000,000 represents the total estimated cost, not the current liability. Only the portion corresponding to capacity used to date is recognized as a liability.
Wrong (D): $13,800,000 does not correspond to a valid calculation under GASB 18. The liability is strictly proportional to the percentage of capacity consumed.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11434;

-- Q11437: State and Local Government [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under GASB standards and modified accrual, short-term revenue anticipation notes are reported as fund liabilities because they will be repaid from current financial resources within the fiscal year. The liability is reduced upon repayment, and interest is recognized as an expenditure when due.
Wrong (A): RAN proceeds are borrowings, not revenue. Recording them as revenue would overstate the fund''s resources and misrepresent the nature of the transaction.
Wrong (B): Other financing source treatment applies to long-term debt proceeds, not short-term borrowings that create a current fund liability. RANs mature within the fiscal year.
Wrong (C): RAN proceeds are not deferred inflows. Deferred inflows represent resources acquired but not yet available or earned, whereas RANs create an immediate liability to repay borrowed funds.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11437;

-- Q11439: State and Local Government [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under GASB 10 (par. 62-64), internal service fund charges should approximate the cost of providing coverage, including estimated claims and IBNR. A persistent surplus averaging $900,000 annually indicates premiums exceed actual costs, overstating departmental expenditures and potentially distorting function-level expenses in government-wide statements.
Wrong (A): While some reserve buildup is prudent, a persistent surplus of $900,000 per year over three years exceeds reasonable reserve needs. GASB 10 requires charges to approximate actual costs, not to accumulate excessive surpluses.
Wrong (B): While the conclusion that premiums are too high is directionally correct, stating rates ''must be lowered immediately'' is too prescriptive. The appropriate conclusion is that rates should be reduced, with adjustments based on actuarial analysis.
Wrong (D): Internal service fund transactions are not eliminated entirely. While ISF markup is eliminated, the underlying cost allocation to individual governmental functions is affected by premium levels, making pricing relevant.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11439;

-- Q11441: State and Local Government [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under GASB 46 and GASB 34 (par. 34), resources constrained by enabling legislation are classified as restricted net position as long as the legislation remains in effect. The hotel tax statute is currently enforceable, making the $5,800,000 restricted.
Wrong (B): The mere possibility of future repeal does not remove the restriction. GASB 46 provides that restricted classification applies when enabling legislation is legally enforceable, regardless of the legislature''s theoretical power to change the law.
Wrong (C): Committed net position results from the government''s highest level of decision-making authority imposing a constraint, not from enabling legislation. Enabling legislation creates a restriction, which is a stronger classification.
Wrong (D): Assigned net position reflects intended use established by the governing body or a designee. Statutory dedication through enabling legislation is a legally enforceable restriction, not merely an assignment.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11441;

-- Q11442: State and Local Government [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under GASB 10 (par. 2), a public entity risk pool is a cooperative group of governmental entities joining together to pool risks and resources to finance risk of loss. It may operate as a separate entity or as part of a participating government.
Wrong (A): A private insurance company is a commercial entity, not a cooperative governmental arrangement. Public entity risk pools are formed by governments themselves, not by private insurers.
Wrong (B): A federal disaster reimbursement program is a separate intergovernmental arrangement, not a cooperative pooling of risks among peer governments.
Wrong (C): An internal service fund is a single government''s internal risk financing mechanism. It differs from a public entity risk pool, which involves multiple separate governments cooperatively sharing risk.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11442;

-- Q11443: State and Local Government [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under GASB 34, government-wide statements use full accrual, which recognizes imposed nonexchange revenue in the period levied regardless of availability. The $800,000 due in April is recognized as revenue in government-wide statements but deferred in the fund statements, creating an $800,000 reconciliation adjustment.
Wrong (A): Full accrual eliminates the availability criterion, so the $800,000 is recognized in government-wide statements even though it is deferred at the fund level. A reconciliation adjustment is required.
Wrong (C): The $800,000 is a valid receivable expected to be collected. It does not reduce net position or require an allowance; it is recognized as revenue under full accrual.
Wrong (D): The $800,000 is a receivable and revenue under full accrual, not a deferred outflow of resources. Deferred outflows are a distinct element unrelated to property tax timing differences.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11443;

-- Q11447: State and Local Government [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under GASB 86 and IRC Section 148, advance refundings create economic costs beyond face value savings: the $1,200,000 in additional debt (escrowing $21,200,000 vs. $20,000,000 outstanding), negative arbitrage on the escrow, and potential arbitrage rebate obligations may offset or exceed the $320,000 annual savings. Waiting for the 2028 call date avoids these costs.
Wrong (A): The $1,200,000 premium and arbitrage costs are not simply amortized away. These real economic costs reduce or eliminate the apparent savings, making advance refunding less attractive than it appears.
Wrong (B): While avoiding arbitrage issues is directionally correct, this choice omits the $1,200,000 additional debt and provides an incomplete analysis of why current refunding is preferable.
Wrong (C): While defeased debt is removed from the statement of net position, the new refunding bonds replace it. Improved debt ratios alone do not justify the economic costs of advance refunding.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11447;

-- Q11453: State and Local Government [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under GASB 86 (par. 3-5), an in-substance defeasance through an irrevocable trust removes the debt from the statement of net position. However, the government must continue disclosing the amount of defeased debt outstanding in the notes until the bonds are fully retired.
Wrong (A): Once the defeasance criteria are met through an irrevocable trust with matched cash flows, the debt is removed from the statement of net position. It does not remain on the face of the statements until maturity.
Wrong (B): The $200,000 excess escrow is not recognized as a current-period gain. The defeasance transaction is reported based on GASB 86 requirements, and the excess is part of the escrow arrangement.
Wrong (C): The note disclosure requirement persists for the life of the defeased bonds, not just through the defeasance date. Ongoing disclosure is required until the bonds are fully retired.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11453;

-- Q11455: State and Local Government [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under GASB 62 (par. 63-68), a change in depreciation method is classified as a change in accounting principle requiring retroactive application. The $340,000 cumulative effect is reported as a restatement of beginning net position for the earliest period presented.
Wrong (A): The cumulative effect is not recognized as a current-year expense adjustment. GASB 62 requires retroactive restatement, not prospective recognition of the cumulative difference.
Wrong (C): Deferred outflows of resources are unrelated to depreciation method changes. The $340,000 cumulative effect is a restatement adjustment, not a deferred item to be amortized.
Wrong (D): Under GASB, a change in depreciation method is a change in accounting principle, not a change in estimate. This differs from FASB guidance (ASU 2015-01), which treats it as an estimate change applied prospectively.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11455;

-- Q11490: Special Purpose Frameworks [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C Section 800, a special purpose framework (SPF) was historically called an other comprehensive basis of accounting (OCBOA). The AICPA replaced the OCBOA terminology with SPF in the clarified auditing standards, but the older term remains widely recognized in practice.
Wrong (A): ''Simplified reporting standard'' is not a recognized term in professional accounting literature. It is a fabricated designation with no basis in AICPA or FASB standards.
Wrong (C): ''Nonauthoritative reporting framework'' is not an established professional term. Special purpose frameworks are recognized in authoritative auditing standards, not classified as nonauthoritative.
Wrong (D): ''Alternative measurement basis'' is not a term used in professional accounting standards to describe cash-basis or other special purpose frameworks.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11490;
